[Event "Kings Gambit: the gambit itself"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/3snUMYzJ"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Today we play the Kings Gambit. You throw a pawn away on purpose, grab the middle, and open a column at his king. Start with the king pawn. [%cal Ye2e4] } 1. e4 { Your pawn marches to e4 and claims the centre. He has to answer it before he does anything else. } (1. d4 { A fine first move, and it leads to a whole different kind of game. } 1... Nf6 { That is the queen pawn game, calm and strategic. Today we play the open, swashbuckling kind. }) 1... e5 { He answers in the centre too. Now for the gambit itself: push your f pawn two squares, to f4. [%cal Yf2f4] } 2. f4 { The Kings Gambit. The pawn is offered to drag his pawn off e5, so you can own the centre with d4 and swing a rook down the f column later. [%csl Re5] } (2. Nf3 { A completely sound way to meet e5, and a real weapon on its own. Just not today's adventure. } 2... Nc6 { That road leads to the Ruy Lopez and the Italian. We want something wilder today. }) (2. Bc4 { A fine developing move, already aiming at f7. But the gambit comes first. } 2... Nf6 { A normal, quiet game follows, sound in every way and far calmer than the gambit. }) (2. Nc3 { Also perfectly good, a natural developing move. But pushing the f pawn is the whole idea today. } 2... Nf6 { That is the Vienna, solid and well tested. A good opening, and a different lesson. }) 2... exf4 { He grabs the pawn. The gambit is accepted. Now bring your king knight out to f3. [%cal Yg1f3] } 3. Nf3 { The knight jumps to f3 and covers h4, so his queen has no check on that square. [%csl Rh4][%cal Gf3h4] } (3. Bc4 { The Bishop's Gambit, just as sound as ours. But it lets his queen check you on h4 at once. } 3... c6 4. Nc3 Qh4+ 5. Kf1 d5 { Playable, and the bishop hits d5. But your king has walked to f1 and can never castle now. [%csl Rf1] }) (3. Nc3 { Also fine, a natural developing move. But f3 is where this knight really wants to stand, guarding h4. [%cal Bg1f3,Bf3h4] } 3... Nc6 4. Nf3 g5 { A move later you reach a position like ours, with a knight out on each side. }) (3. d4 { Grabbing the centre looks tempting, but his queen checks first and costs you a whole move. } 3... Qh4+ 4. Ke2 Qe7 5. Kf2 Nf6 { Your king has walked to f2 by hand, and it can never castle. Not the game we want. [%csl Rf2] }) 3... g5 { He plants pawns on g5 and f4 to hold his extra pawn. Poke a hole in that wall: push your rook pawn to h4. [%cal Yh2h4] } 4. h4 { You knock on his pawn wall. If he ever takes on h4, the h column swings open toward his king. [%csl Rg5] } (4. d4 { A very good move too, grabbing the centre while it is free. But we want to crack his wall open. } 4... d6 { A slower fight. The rook pawn push opens things up far more quickly. }) (4. Nc3 { Sound, but it lets his pawn run on and take your knight. } 4... d6 5. d4 g4 6. Bxf4 gxf3 7. Qxf3 { You gave a knight for two pawns, and he keeps the better game. The rook pawn push stops all of that. }) (4. Bc4 { Tempting, the bishop stares at f7 already. But the g pawn keeps rolling forward. } 4... g4 5. O-O gxf3 6. Qxf3 Qf6 { Your queen took back one pawn, but the knight on f3 is gone. A piece for two pawns is not enough. }) 4... g4 { His pawn on g4 attacks your knight. Do not let it be taken for nothing: jump it forward to e5. [%cal Yf3e5] } 5. Ne5 { Well posted. Your knight lands on e5, safe from his pawns, and stares straight at f7. That is the Kieseritzky position. [%cal Ge5f7] } (5. d4 { His pawn on g4 simply takes your knight, and your queen only wins the pawn back. } 5... gxf3 6. Qxf3 Nc6 7. c3 d6 { A knight gone for a single pawn. Your queen on f3 cannot bring it back. }) (5. Ng5 { A bold jump at f7. But one pawn push chases it into trouble. } 5... h6 6. Nxf7 Kxf7 7. d4 f3 { Two pawns for a whole knight, and his king only had to step aside once. Not enough. }) (5. Ng1 { Home again, safe and sad: two moves spent to end up where the knight started. } 5... d5 6. d4 Bh6 7. e5 c5 { He grabs the centre and keeps his extra pawn. Retreating cost you the whole plan. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: the Kieseritzky main line"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/xYOugfFE"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1p1p/8/4N3/4PppP/8/PPPP2P1/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 1 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1p1p/8/4N3/4PppP/8/PPPP2P1/RNBQKB1R_b_KQkq_-_1_5"]

{ The Kieseritzky position: your knight sits deep on e5, and his pawns on f4 and g4 stand in front of his king. }
5... Nf6 { He develops his knight to f6, and it hits your pawn on e4. What do you do about it? } 6. d4 { Nice. The pawn backs up your knight on e5 and claims the centre in one move. [%csl Gd4,Ge5] } (6. Nxg4 { A pawn grab, and a good move. But the big centre with d4 serves you better. } 6... Nxe4 { Material is level, the position is tidy, and your attack has fizzled out. }) (6. Bc4 { Playable, developing with a threat on f7. But your knight on e5 still stands alone. } 6... d5 7. exd5 Bd6 8. d4 Nh5 { His pawn on f4 is safe, and his pieces came out with tempo on yours. }) (6. Be2 { Too shy. Your knight on e5 still has no support. } 6... Nxe4 7. d4 Ng3 8. Rh2 d6 { Two pieces are hit at once: his pawn attacks your knight on e5, his knight attacks your bishop on e2. One of them falls. [%cal Rd6e5,Rg3e2] }) 6... d6 { His d pawn kicks your knight. Where can it go and still do a job? } 7. Nd3 { Well spotted. From d3 the knight is safe and leans on the pawn you are chasing, on f4. [%cal Gd3f4] } (7. Bxf4 { Careful: he just takes your knight on e5 with his pawn. } 7... dxe5 8. Bxe5 Nbd7 9. Bf4 Nh5 { A knight for a single pawn. Stepping back to d3 kept it. }) (7. Nxf7 { The classic sacrifice. Here it only buys two pawns for the knight. } 7... Kxf7 8. Bxf4 Be6 9. Qd2 Rg8 { His king is safe on f7, and you are simply a piece short. }) (7. Nc4 { The knight steps aside, but on c4 it does nothing, a wasted move. } 7... Bh6 8. Nc3 Nh5 { His knight on h5 and bishop on h6 both guard f4, and you have no plan left. [%cal Rh5f4,Rh6f4] }) 7... Nxe4 { He grabs your pawn on e4. How do you win a pawn straight back? } 8. Bxf4 { Lovely. Your bishop takes on f4 and aims at d6. He is a pawn up, but your pieces are out and his king has lost its pawn cover. [%cal Gf4d6] } (8. Qe2 { Also good: the queen stares down the e column. The bishop takes on f4 a move later. } 8... Qe7 { The same kind of game, a move slower than taking on f4 at once. }) (8. Nxf4 { The wrong piece takes back. Your knight leaves d3, and your bishop is still at home. } 8... Be7 9. Bd3 Bxh4+ { His bishop takes your h pawn with check. The bishop was the right piece to take on f4. [%cal Re7h4] }) (8. Nc3 { Solid, but it lets his knight jump to g3 with threats. } 8... Ng3 9. Rh2 Qe7+ 10. Kf2 Bg7 { Your king is stuck in the middle, and he has the attack now. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: he hits back with the d pawn"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/rbS1fAhY"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq f3 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_f3_0_2"]

{ You have offered the f pawn. His pawn on e5 can take it, or he can do something else. }
2... d5 { He turns the pawn down and strikes back in the centre with his d pawn. Take it, or let his centre stand? } 3. exd5 { Yes, take it. Never let him keep a free pawn and a big centre both at once. } (3. Nf3 { Also excellent. If he takes on f4, you take on d5 and reach our position. } 3... dxe4 4. Nxe5 Nd7 { But he took on e4 instead, and your gambit pawn plan is gone. Level, and a different game. }) (3. fxe5 { Look again: his queen has a clear road to h4. } 3... Qh4+ 4. g3 Qxe4+ 5. Qe2 Qxh1 { Your rook in the corner is gone. Taking on d5 first avoids the whole mess. }) (3. Nc3 { Playable, but he pushes his pawn past yours and cramps you. } 3... d4 4. Nce2 Nc6 { His pawn wedge on d4 blocks your knight and your d pawn for the rest of the game. }) (3. d4 { Grabbing more centre looks tempting, but his pieces come out with tempo. } 3... exd4 4. Qxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 { Your queen on d4 gets chased, and his knights are out first. }) 3... exf4 { Now he takes the f pawn after all. Which piece comes out first, before his queen finds a check? } 4. Nf3 { Good. The knight covers h4, so his queen has no check there. [%csl Rh4][%cal Gf3h4] } (4. Bb5+ { A tempting check, but he blocks it and develops with tempo on your bishop. } 4... c6 5. Qe2+ Be7 6. dxc6 bxc6 { His pieces come out on yours, and your pawn on d5 is gone. }) (4. d4 { The big centre looks right, but h4 is still open. } 4... Qh4+ 5. Kd2 Qd8 { Your king has had to walk to d2, and you stand clearly worse. }) (4. Qf3 { The queen guards nothing out there and blocks her own knight. } 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 Bd6 { He develops calmly while your queen is in the way. }) (4. Bc4 { Tempting: the bishop stares at f7. But the check comes first. } 4... Qh4+ 5. Kf1 { Your king has walked to f1 and can never castle. The knight to f3 sidesteps all of this. [%csl Rf1] }) 4... Nf6 { His knight goes after your extra pawn on d5. Can you hold onto it? } 5. c4 { Yes. The c pawn props d5 up from behind, and your extra pawn holds. [%cal Gc4d5] } (5. Bc4 { A good move too. You hand the pawn back but develop fast. } 5... Nxd5 { Level, and the extra pawn has gone back to him. }) (5. Bb5+ { Also good: the check buys a free moment. } 5... c6 6. dxc6 Nxc6 { Equal and pleasant, but the c pawn plan keeps the extra pawn instead. }) (5. Nc3 { Natural development, but the pawn on d5 now falls for free. } 5... Nxd5 6. Nxd5 Qxd5 { Your extra pawn is gone and his queen sits in the middle. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: he declines with the bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/IRvxW008"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq f3 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_f3_0_2"]

{ You have offered the f pawn, and his king side bishop is still on f8. }
2... Bc5 { He turns the pawn down and points his bishop straight at your king side. Be careful here. } 3. Nf3 { Right. The knight covers h4, so his queen cannot team up with the bishop there. [%csl Rh4][%cal Gf3h4] } (3. fxe5 { Greedy, and it opens a road for his queen. } 3... Qh4+ 4. g3 Qxe4+ 5. Qe2 Qxh1 { Your rook in the corner is gone. That is why the knight comes first. }) (3. Nc3 { Every bit as sound as the knight to f3, just less pointed. } 3... d6 { Much the same kind of position by a slightly different road. }) (3. d3 { Solid, but slow, and h4 is still unguarded. } 3... d6 { Playable, though he gets a comfortable game from your delay. }) 3... d6 { He props up his e pawn with d6. His bishop on c5 is his best piece. Can a pawn hit it? } 4. c3 { Nice. The c pawn prepares d4, and d4 will hit his bishop. [%cal Gc3d4] } (4. Nc3 { Plain, honest development, nothing wrong with it. } 4... Nf6 { A normal game. But we want to hit that bishop with a pawn. }) (4. Bc4 { Playable, and now the two bishops stare at each other. } 4... Nf6 { Even and quiet. The pawn plan against his bishop is sharper. }) (4. fxe5 { You can take, but it helps him finish developing. } 4... dxe5 { Equal, and your gambit pawn on f4 no longer does anything. }) 4... Nf6 { His knight comes to f6 and hits your pawn on e4. What was c3 for? } 5. d4 { There it is. The pawn hits his bishop, and your pawns fill the centre. [%cal Gd4c5] } (5. fxe5 { Also good: take first, then push d4 next. } 5... dxe5 { The f column is open, and d4 is still coming. A different road to the same centre. }) (5. Bb5+ { The check changes nothing. } 5... Bd7 { He blocks with his bishop, and your d pawn still has to come. }) (5. Be2 { A quiet developing move. } 5... O-O { He castles, and your centre push has lost its sting. d4 at once was sharper. }) 5... exd4 { He takes on d4. Two of your pieces can take back. Which one? } 6. cxd4 { Exactly. The c pawn takes back and hits his bishop again. } (6. Nxd4 { The knight takes, but now no pawn hits his bishop, so e4 is free. } 6... Nxe4 { His knight grabs your pawn on e4, and your centre is gone. [%cal Rf6e4] }) (6. e5 { Pushing past looks strong, but he takes on e5. } 6... dxe5 7. fxe5 Nd5 { His knight sits on d5, his pawn on d4 stands, and your centre is gone. }) (6. Bd3 { You forgot to take back. } 6... dxc3 7. Nxc3 { He has taken a second pawn on c3, and you have nothing for it. }) 6... Bb6 { His bishop is chased back to b6. Bring out your queen knight. } 7. Nc3 { Your pawns on d4 and e4 stand side by side, his bishop was driven off c5, and your knight guards e4. The centre is yours. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (7. e5 { Also good: push on, and his knight has to move. } 7... Nd5 { A sharp game, level. The knight to c3 keeps it simple. }) (7. Bd3 { Also good, guarding e4 with the bishop. [%cal Gd3e4] } 7... O-O { Fine either way. The knight to c3 comes first in our line. }) (7. Bb5+ { The check chases nothing. } 7... c6 { His pawn blocks and hits your bishop, which must move again. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: the Fischer defence"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/dU5DOQyZ"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/8/4Pp2/5N2/PPPP2PP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 1 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/8/4Pp2/5N2/PPPP2PP/RNBQKB1R_b_KQkq_-_1_3"]

{ Gambit accepted: his pawn sits on f4, your knight stands on f3, and his pawns on d7 and g7 have not moved yet. }
3... d6 { The Fischer defence: a calm d6 instead of the pawn wall. There is no weakness yet. } 4. Bc4 { Good pick. The bishop flies out and glares down the diagonal at f7. [%cal Gc4f7] } (4. d4 { Also strong: the whole centre instead of a piece first. } 4... g5 { A good game too, though the bishop was hoping to reach f7 sooner. }) (4. h4 { Stopping his pawn push early. Reasonable and patient. } 4... Nf6 { Sound, but your bishop is still at home. }) (4. Nc3 { It lets him build the pawn wall and push on. } 4... g5 5. Bc4 g4 6. O-O gxf3 7. Qxf3 { A whole knight gone for a pawn and an attack. Too much. }) 4... h6 { He plays h6, getting ready to push his g pawn. Can you stop that plan? } 5. h4 { Yes. If his g pawn ever comes to g5, you take twice and your rook lands on h8. [%csl Rg5][%cal Gh4g5,Gh1h8] } (5. d4 { Natural, but his pawn wall goes up for free. } 5... g5 { You play the rook pawn push a move later anyway. }) (5. Nc3 { Fine development, but the g pawn now comes with tempo. } 5... g5 { Playable, though a shade less pleasant than stopping the wall first. }) (5. O-O { Castling is sensible, but the pawn wall goes up for free. } 5... g5 6. c3 Nc6 7. d4 Nf6 { He keeps his extra pawn on f4 behind the wall, and you have no attack for it. }) 5... Nf6 { His knight aims at your pawn on e4. Who guards it? } 6. Nc3 { Perfect. The knight guards your pawn on e4 and eyes d5. [%cal Gc3e4] } (6. d4 { Nobody guards your pawn on e4 now. } 6... Nxe4 7. Qe2 Qe7 8. Bxf4 Nf6 { A pawn given for nothing: the knight to c3 would have kept it. }) (6. O-O { Castling is nice, but your pawn on e4 still hangs. } 6... Nh5 7. d4 g5 { He holds f4 with his knight and his pawn, and you have nothing for the gambit. }) (6. d3 { Solid, but slow, and it leaves f4 for him. } 6... Nh5 7. Nc3 Be7 { He is comfortable and a clean pawn up. }) 1-0


[Event "Kings Gambit: the early queen check"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/rjyt79qq/IRuK3zgO"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq f3 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/8/4p3/4PP2/8/PPPP2PP/RNBQKBNR_b_KQkq_f3_0_2"]

{ You have offered the f pawn. With it gone from f2, the diagonal from h4 to your king on e1 is open. }
2... Qh4+ { Out comes his queen with check. Only two moves answer it here. Pick the right one. } 3. g3 { The right one. The pawn blocks and hits his queen, so she must move again. [%cal Gg3h4] } (3. Ke2 { Your king walks, and your castling is gone for good. } 3... Bc5 4. Qe1 Qxf4 5. d3 Qf6 { He took your pawn on f4, and your king stands in the open on e2. }) 3... Qe7 { His queen backs off to e7, blocking his own bishop on f8. Your move. } 4. Nc3 { Good. Develop, and leave the pawn on f4 alone for now: his queen is in her own bishop's way. [%csl Rf8] } (4. d3 { Also good and sensible. } 4... d5 { Pleasant for you, but he trades a pair of centre pawns and gets moving. }) (4. Bg2 { Fine too, though the knight does more right now. } 4... d5 { Still nice for you, a touch slower than our line. }) (4. c4 { Grabs space, and playable. But develop first. } 4... exf4 { He takes on f4 at once, and your development is behind. }) 4... exf4 { At last he takes the f pawn. What is your strongest pawn push? } 5. d4 { Yes. Two pawns abreast in the centre, and his queen is stuck in front of her own bishop. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (5. gxf4 { You can take back, but it cracks open the pawns in front of your own king. } 5... d5 6. Qe2 dxe4 7. Nxe4 Nc6 { Your edge has slipped away. The centre push was stronger. }) (5. Bg2 { A good move too: the bishop takes the long diagonal. [%cal Gg2b7] } 5... fxg3 { Still comfortable, though the centre push hits harder. }) (5. Qf3 { The queen comes out early and can be chased. } 5... c6 { Fine for you, and messier than the clean centre push. }) 5... fxg3 { He takes on g3, right beside your king. Which pawn takes back? } 6. hxg3 { Well spotted. The h column opens, and your rook on h1 stares straight down it at his pawn on h7. [%cal Gh1h7] } (6. Bf4 { Also excellent: develop first and take on g3 later. } 6... Nf6 { Just as good. Both roads open the same h column. }) (6. Qf3 { Also fine, but that pawn on g3 deserves to be taken at once. } 6... d6 { Still pleasant for you, just a little untidy. }) (6. Nh3 { The knight goes to the rim just to guard the pawn. } 6... Nf6 7. Bg2 gxh2 { His pawn crawls on to h2 and your edge has disappeared. }) 1-0
